 aligned you can make a  crease on the outside and then we could  tear off or cut off this excess paper  here if you're using square paper then  you don't need to make this diagonal  fold so you could begin here take the  top edge now and then you could fold it  down to the bottom edge once it's neatly  lined up then you can make a crease  along the top of the paper  now you can open up the paper and you'll  see that we have this horizontal line  here in the middle we're going to rotate  the paper so that that line is now  vertical and then we're going to take  both of the top corners and fold them  directly into the middle just like we  would for an airplane so just fold it in  so that the top is aligned with that  middle line then you can make a crease  on the outside do the same with the left  side take the top corner fold it  directly into the middle and then make a  crease we're also going to do this for  both of the bottom corners so if you  want to rotate it around just like that  then you could do the same take the top  corner fold it in and then make a crease  on the outside the last one fold it in  and then make a crease now what we're  going to do is we're going to take both  of these bottom edges here and we're  going to fold them directly into the  middle on that vertical line also so you  want to take this bottom edge and fold  it in you might need to round this off a  little bit and just fold it in like this  so that this corner is also directly in  the middle once you line it up neatly  then you can make a crease on the  outside do the same with the other  bottom edge fold it in line it up with  the middle  and then make a crease on the outside so  now it should look something like this  now what we're going to do is we're  going to turn over the model and we're  going to fold this point down until we  see these edges align with the top of  the crease that we're going to make so  we basically want to crease it along  this the top of these two edges so flip  it over fold it down make sure it lines  up with the top of those two edges and  also the tip of the paper should be  directly on that vertical crease once  it's like that then you can make a  crease along the top so it should look  like this  then what we're going to do is we're  going to take both sides of the paper  and we're going to fold them down away  from us just like that just want to take  both edges fold them down and then make  a crease and make sure the entire piece  is nice and flat and that's it that's  how to make one of the units I'm going  to quickly go through with the red one  if you want to see how they're  interlocked all you need to do is to go  to this time down here take the top edge  and fold it down to the bottom edge make  a crease open it up fold both of these  corners into that vertical line  for both of the bottom corners now into  the middle  fold both of the bottom edges into the  middle  flip the paper over and then you're  going to fold this tip down until the  edge is flush or aligned at the top of  these two edges there make a crease and  then fold both sides down away from you  once you finish making all 16 units then  you're ready to interlock them and I  didn't say this before but if you do  want it to have alternating colors like  the one that I have you just need to  make eight of one color and eight of  another color if you take a look at the  pieces you'll notice that each one has  two pockets here and they also have two  tips you're going to take the tips of  one piece and slide them into the  pockets of another one so just kind of  squeeze down on it like that  and make sure that both of them go into  the pockets of the other piece and then  you could push it in all the way until  it doesn't go anymore  then again I'm going to take a blue  piece now take it squeeze it together  until both of the tips go into the  pockets and then push it in all the way  and that's how to interlock it it's  really easy and I think we should  probably fast-forward this a little bit  [Music]  so once you've finished interlocking  them all then we just need to make sure  that the last one fits in properly then  you just need to round it off by kind of  pushing all around just like this so as  you'll notice if you do move it around a  little bit it's going to come apart so  what I'm going to do is to tape it  together and the way that I do this is I  just put some tape on the inside of the  ninja star in this middle ring and I'm  going to put it sideways so I'm sure you  guys can figure this out you know you  don't have to watch me do the whole  thing so let's speed it up a little bit  [Music]  all right so there you go as you can see  I used eight pieces of tape you just  want to make sure that each of these  individual joints are taped together you  might want to use the transparent tape  because it looks a little nicer but  there you go now it's stuck together so  when I throw it I found that if you  throw it vertically by taking it in  between two of your fingers like this  and flicking it out like that it  actually flies pretty good so let's give  it a shot  it also flies really good just throwing  it over hand like this if you want to  try throwing it with your hand in the  middle like this like a disc  that'll work also I hope you guys enjoy  the video be sure to rate and subscribe  sometimes you might be wondering if you  can get more than 16 pieces in the knit  ninja star and the answer is yes you can  I actually put 20 pieces together but  let me know how many you're able to get  I have some ideas and requests for  tutorials that I'd like to do in the  future but leave me a comment below let  me know what you'd like to see the most  I also want to get my portrait video out  that my friend did and I'm also going to  be updating my Rob's phone channel  really soon so be sure to check it out  so where was I for the last couple of  months well a lot of people came up with  some really creative comments on my last  video and here are the most popular I'll  see you later guys  [Music]  you

